UPDATE hotels SET
  article = $ART$<p><strong>The Oberoi Sahl Hasheesh</strong> is a Red Sea resort south of Hurghada, and it earns its place on an Egypt trip for one reason: it is where the temples part stops.</p>

<p>Every suite on the property has its own private courtyard or garden, and some have private pools.</p>

<p>There are no standard rooms here at all.</p>

<p>The architecture is Moorish, built around domes and courtyards rather than corridors, and the effect is that the resort reads as a series of private enclosures rather than as a single large building.</p>

<p>That matters more than it sounds after eight days of coaches, tomb queues and six in the morning starts.</p>

<h2>Why Stay at the Oberoi Sahl Hasheesh?</h2>

<p>Because the last three nights of an Egypt itinerary are the ones most people get wrong.</p>

<p>The classic route through Cairo, Luxor and Aswan is dense, early and physically demanding, and travellers routinely fly home straight off the back of it feeling as though they need a holiday.</p>

<p>A stay here is the correction, and it works because the property is built for stopping rather than for doing.</p>

<p>A private courtyard means you can be outside without being in public, which is the specific thing a resort pool does not give you and the specific thing people want when they are tired.</p>

<p>The private beach gives the stay a centre of gravity that does not involve a schedule.</p>

<p>Who it does not suit is the traveller who wants the beach days to be busy.</p>

<p>If you are looking for a large watersports operation, a lively bar scene or a resort with several hundred rooms and the energy that comes with them, the Hurghada strip north of here will serve you better and cost you less.</p>

<h2>Where Sahl Hasheesh Sits</h2>

<p>It is south of Hurghada, far enough out that the town's noise and traffic are not part of the stay.</p>

<p>That distance is the point and it is also the trade.</p>

<p>You are buying quiet, and what you give up is the ability to walk out of the gate and find a town.</p>

<p>The airport at Hurghada is close enough to make this a practical end to an itinerary rather than an expedition, which is why it works as a decompression stay and would not if it were another three hours down the coast.</p>

<p>Internal flights from Luxor and Aswan connect through easily, so the beach leg does not cost a travel day the way a long road transfer would.</p>

<h2>Against Three More Nights on the River</h2>

<p>The honest comparison is not with another Red Sea hotel, it is with the alternative use of the same three nights.</p>

<p>Most travellers choosing this are choosing between a beach stay and extending the Nile leg, either with more nights on the water or more nights in Aswan.</p>

<p>More river time gives you more of what you came for, and it gives you more early mornings, more moving and more sites.</p>

<p>The beach gives you none of those and gives back the thing the rest of the trip spent.</p>

<p>The rule of thumb is simple: if this is a first visit to Egypt and the trip already runs eight days or more, the beach has earned its nights.</p>

<p>If it is a second visit, or the trip is short, the river is the better use of the nights and this property is the wrong call.</p>

<h2>Suites</h2>

<p>The whole property is suites, and the decision is about what is private to you outside the room rather than about the room itself.</p>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Suite type</th><th>What is private to you</th><th>Who it suits</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Suite with a private courtyard</td><td>An enclosed outdoor courtyard belonging to the suite, walled and not overlooked</td><td>Couples who want to be outside without being in public</td></tr>
<tr><td>Suite with a private garden</td><td>Planted private outdoor space rather than a walled courtyard</td><td>Guests who want green space and room to move outside the room</td></tr>
<tr><td>Suite with a private pool</td><td>Everything above plus a pool belonging only to the suite</td><td>Travellers who will not use the main pool or the beach at all</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Which Suite to Choose</h3>

<p>For most people finishing a temples itinerary, a suite with a private courtyard is the right answer and the private pool is money spent on something you will use twice.</p>

<p>The exception is real and worth naming: if you are travelling in the hotter months, or with a group that will not want to walk to the beach in the middle of the day, the private pool changes the stay entirely.</p>

<p>It also changes it for anyone who values not being seen, which after a week of group sites is a more common preference than people admit.</p>

<p>Ask how far the suite sits from the beach, because on a property built around courtyards the walk varies more than a floor plan suggests.</p>

<p>And ask which direction the courtyard faces, because a courtyard that gets sun until four is a different proposition from one that loses it at noon.</p>

<h2>The Beach and the Resort</h2>

<p>The private beach is the centre of the property and the reason the suites are arranged the way they are.</p>

<p>There is a spa, a pool, restaurants and a gym, and the scale is set by the number of suites rather than by the number of activities.</p>

<p>The Red Sea here is warm and clear for most of the year, and the snorkelling is close enough to shore to be a decision you make after breakfast rather than an excursion you book.</p>

<p>The Moorish domes and courtyards do practical work as well as visual work, because they shade the walkways and keep the public areas usable in the middle of the day.</p>

<p>The house reef is the part of this stay that surprises people who came only to lie still.</p>

<p>Being able to put a mask on and see something worth seeing without getting on a boat is a different proposition from a dive trip, and it is the one activity here that fits a decompression stay rather than fighting it.</p>

<h2>Practical Notes</h2>

<p>The Red Sea season is longer than the Nile Valley season, which is exactly why this leg goes at the end.</p>

<p>The months that are too hot for Luxor are still workable here because the sea moderates the air and there is somewhere to swim.</p>

<p>Winter is comfortable on land and cooler in the water than most Americans expect from the phrase Red Sea.</p>

<p>Wind is the variable nobody plans for, and there are afternoons on this coast when the sea gets up and the beach stops being the point.</p>

<p>A suite with its own sheltered courtyard is the hedge against exactly that afternoon.</p>

<p>Three nights is the number that works, two feels like a stopover and four starts to feel like a separate holiday attached to the wrong trip.</p>

<p>We book the suite category and the courtyard aspect together, because the outdoor space is the product here and it is the part a booking system will not describe accurately.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Is a private pool suite at the oberoi sahl hasheesh worth it?</h3>
<p>For most guests finishing a temples itinerary, no, and a suite with a private courtyard is the better use of the difference.</p>
<p>It becomes worth it in the hotter months, or for travellers who will not use the main pool or the beach at all, because then the private pool is the whole outdoor life of the stay.</p>

<h3>Do all the suites have private outdoor space?</h3>
<p>Yes, every suite has either a private courtyard or a private garden, and some also have a private pool.</p>
<p>There are no standard rooms on the property at all, which is why the choice here is about what is outside the suite rather than about the bedroom.</p>

<h3>How many nights should I add on the Red Sea?</h3>
<p>Three, and that number holds across almost every trip that finishes on this coast.</p>
<p>Two nights feels like a stopover once you have counted the arrival and departure halves, and four starts to feel like a separate holiday attached to the end of the wrong trip.</p>

<h3>How far is it from Hurghada airport?</h3>
<p>It sits south of Hurghada, close enough to the airport to work as the end of an itinerary rather than as an expedition in its own right.</p>
<p>Internal flights from Luxor and Aswan connect through easily, so the beach leg does not cost a travel day the way a long road transfer would.</p>

<h3>Should I do this or more nights on the Nile?</h3>
<p>If this is a first visit and the itinerary already runs eight days or more, the beach earns its place because the rest of the trip is dense and early.</p>
<p>If it is a second visit, or the trip is short, more river time is the better use of the same nights and this is the wrong property for you.</p>

<h3>What are the best months to come?</h3>
<p>The Red Sea season is longer than the Nile Valley season, which is the reason this leg belongs at the end of a trip rather than the start.</p>
<p>The months that are too hot for Luxor still work here because the sea moderates the air, and winter is comfortable on land while the water is cooler than most visitors expect.</p>$ART$,
